<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dietrich Klagges</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1891">1891</a>–<a href="/years/1971">1971</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dietrich Klagges was a German Nazi politician who served as interior minister of the Free State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> from 1931 and as its minister-president from 1933 to 1945. In February 1932 he appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> to a post in Brunswick's legation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, giving the stateless Hitler German citizenship so that he could run for president. After the war he was convicted for crimes committed under his rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dietrich-klagges.jpg" alt="Dietrich Klagges" width="320" height="417" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Artmann, Braunschweig · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dietrich_Klagges.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Klagges, born on 1 February 1891, was the youngest of a forest ranger's seven children and trained as a teacher at a seminar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soest">Soest</a>. Discharged from military service in 1911 because of an ear infection, he re-enlisted in 1915, was badly wounded at Neuve Chapelle on 1 April 1915 and left the army in 1916 to return to teaching. He belonged to the German National People's Party from 1918 to 1924, briefly to the German Völkisch Freedom Party, and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a> on 13 June 1925. From 1921 he wrote völkisch, antidemocratic and antisemitic articles.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Dismissed from the Prussian school service in 1930 for his party work, he turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a>, where the Nazis had entered the coalition government. As Interior and Education Minister from 15 September 1931 he imposed professional bans on Social Democrats and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> in the civil service. His first plan to naturalise Hitler, a professorship at the Braunschweig Technical College in an invented subject, leaked and failed. Hitler never forgave him the public ridicule and curbed his power after a visit on 17 July 1935.</p><p>Appointed Ministerpräsident on 6 May 1933, Klagges joined the SS in 1934 and became SS-Obergruppenführer in 1942. He sought to make <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> a Nazi model state, independent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, brought institutions such as the SS Ensigns' School and the German Research Centre for Aviation to Braunschweig, and linked it to the Autobahn and the Mittellandkanal. The Auxiliary Police, answerable directly to him, struck at labour organisations, Social Democrats, Communists and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>. He was responsible for at least 25 deaths, among them eleven communists and labour organisers murdered by the SS at Rieseberg on 4 July 1933. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a> of Braunschweig's Jews began on 21 January 1941.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Taken prisoner by American troops on 12 April 1945, Klagges was sentenced in 1946 by a military court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bielefeld">Bielefeld</a> to six years. In 1950 he was sentenced to life for crimes including the Rieseberg murders; after the Bundesgerichtshof set that aside in 1952, he was resentenced to fifteen years. Released from the Lingen penitentiary on 2 October 1957, he lived in Bad Harzburg, edited right-wing writings, kept contact with neo-Nazi groups and in 1970 won a retroactive pension of about DM 100,000. He died in 1971.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dietrich Klagges” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 1930 the Nazis shared government, and in February 1932 the Nazi interior minister <b>Dietrich Klagges</b> appointed Adolf Hitler to a nominal post in the state's Berlin legation, which made the stateless Hitler a German citizen and allowed him to run for president.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-state-of-brunswick">Free State of Brunswick</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Bad Harzburg, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.88,10.56&amp;year=1943" aria-label="Bad Harzburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4788 370 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Dietrich Klagges"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5238 640h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5238 640h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=51.88,10.56&amp;year=1943">What was here in 1943? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
